  <HOMEWORK title="Bring-Up 8: Fine on the bench, not fine next to a neighbour"
            prompt="The link is fully brought up - CDR, CTLE, and DFE all correctly set the way the earlier steps left them. Before signing off, stress it the way a real validation lab would: with a neighbouring aggressor lane actually running traffic, not just on a quiet bench. A second lane couples into this one at a modest level, slightly offset in rate (a realistic independent-clock aggressor, not a synchronized one)."
            hint="Nothing about the link itself - FFE, CTLE, DFE - changed from the end of the earlier steps. Check the Noise Injector's type and level before assuming the receiver regressed."
            explanation="Nothing about the receiver's own settings changed from where the earlier bring-up steps left them - a crosstalk aggressor at a coupling level of only 0.1 (modest, not an extreme value) is enough to collapse the eye height from about 66% down to under 5% - a margin that was comfortable moments ago is now right at the edge of actually failing, run to run. This is the actual point of a margin stress test: a link that looks completely finished on a quiet bench can still have very little real margin, and a crosstalk-free bring-up pass alone doesn't prove the board will hold up once its neighbours are actually running traffic. (Pushing the coupling level higher still - 0.2 and up, in this same setup - stops the receiver from syncing to the data at all, the more obvious, easy-to-catch version of this failure; the quietly-degraded-but-still-running case shown here is the one that's easy to miss if you only watch the error counter.)"/>
</LABCHAIN>
